{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-114818",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $x=a$ is\n\n$$\nL(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=81,$ which gives\n\n$$\nL(x)=f(81)+{f}^{′}(81)(x-81).\n$$\n\nLet's compute $f(81)$ and ${f}^{′}(81)$ in the expression above. Evaluating the function, we get\n\n$$\nf(81)=6\\sqrt{81}=54,\n$$\n\nand computing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6\\sqrt{x})=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{x}},\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(81)=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{81}}=\\frac{3}{9}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the tangent line approximation is\n\n$$\nL(x)=54+\\frac{1}{3}(x-81).\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $6\\sqrt{87.2}$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=87.2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\sqrt{87.2} & ≈L(87.2) \\\\  & =54+\\frac{1}{3}(87.2-81) \\\\  & =54+\\frac{1}{3}(6.2) \\\\  & =54+\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{31}{5}) \\\\  & =54+\\frac{31}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{841}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
